preparation, you need to properly clean and polish the surface immediately
before etching.

160
00:32:18,312 --> 00:32:28,602
This is to add extra cleaning of the tooth surface to ease the job or the
procedure or the required job from acid etchant, okay?

161
00:32:29,462 --> 00:32:38,302
Some operators prefer also to slightly rough this surface with any finishing
discs just to facilitate the action of acid.

162
00:32:39,122 --> 00:33:00,612
Again, accidental etching of neighboring teeth or uninvolved enamel surfaces
should be avoided as it is difficult to be remineralized again, and the
resultant will be rough enamel surface that will induce discoloration.

163
00:33:00,612 --> 00:33:20,712
Protection of neighboring enamel as we discussed in the previous illustrated
diagrams and photos by rubber dam isolation, teflon tape, Myler strips, all of
these will protect uninvolved enamel surfaces and uninvolved neighboring teeth.

164
00:33:21,392 --> 00:33:24,782
Step number two is resin infiltration into enamel.

165
00:33:25,572 --> 00:33:38,222
Resin infiltration into enamel using a microbrush supplied always with the kit
of bonding agent, just wet it and paint enamel or hd enamel surface.

166
00:33:38,772 --> 00:33:41,232
No, it's not like that.

167
00:33:42,582 --> 00:33:44,822
Number one, you should apply it actively.

168
00:33:44,822 --> 00:33:51,252
Actively means rubbing, rubbing, continuous rubbing, okay?

169
00:33:51,772 --> 00:34:11,472
Continuous rubbing to allow penetration, full penetration of resin to infiltrate
all the formed pores till the bottom of each pore, and you should allow it for
sufficient time to penetrate.

170
00:34:12,172 --> 00:34:16,772
And it is preferable to apply multi coats.

171
00:34:17,832 --> 00:34:38,672
Apply the first coat in active better, let it sufficient time from 15 up to 30
seconds, okay, then apply the second coat, then thinning with a dry brush, then
curing.

172
00:34:39,262 --> 00:34:46,132
This to get the most effective bonding with the tooth structure.

173
00:34:47,362 --> 00:35:07,350
This is a scanning electromicrograph on the left side, showing the microbores
infiltrated with resin tags, okay? And this is the schematic diagrams showing
the micromechanical interlocking between the micro tags and the microbores of
enamel.

174
00:35:07,350 --> 00:35:07,350
Again, this is another photo scanning electron micrograph showing the enamel
infiltrated with resin creating micro tags that solidify and creates the
required micromechanical locking.

175
00:35:07,350 --> 00:35:18,612
The right one is a scanning electromicroscope microscopic photograph or
micrograph showing for the above composite resin, and the bonding agent followed
by the enamel treated with acid or acid etched enamel, okay?

176
00:35:19,102 --> 00:35:31,972
You could notice, as we discussed before in a schematic diagram, the bond
between bonding agent or the adhesion between bonding agent and the enamel is
micromechanical, and between bonding agent and the above composite is chemical.

177
00:35:34,442 --> 00:35:44,722
The adhesion of resin monomer to enamel, as we said before, is a micromechanical
bonding through the formation of resin tags, which becomes interlocked with the
enamel surface.

178
00:35:45,402 --> 00:35:55,032
The bond strength in this case is affected by the degree of diffusion of resin
or infiltration of resin inside enamel.

179
00:35:55,662 --> 00:36:18,392
This is affected strongly by the bore diameter rather than the bore lens,
because the bore diameter is responsible for full penetration of resin inside
the formed bores and escape of air or displacing air instead displacing the air
inside these pores.

180
00:36:19,042 --> 00:36:33,352
While if the bore diameter is narrow and the lens is long, this means that a
back pressure porosity will be created that will prevent proper penetration or
infiltration of the resin inside enamel.

